# name	buffer	point	axis	kind	mark	keys	diverges
bof-w1	hello world\nsecond line\nthird\n	0	-	-	-	w1	-
bof-w2	hello world\nsecond line\nthird\n	0	-	-	-	w2	-
bof-w3	hello world\nsecond line\nthird\n	0	-	-	-	w3	-
bof-w0	hello world\nsecond line\nthird\n	0	-	-	-	w0	-
bof-shiftw2	hello world\nsecond line\nthird\n	0	-	-	-	W2	-
bof-b2	hello world\nsecond line\nthird\n	0	-	-	-	b2	a digit with no selection. Meow raises "No selection": `meow--selection-fallback` looks the fallback up by `this-command`, which is `meow-expand-2`, while `meow-selection-command-fallback`'s key is `meow-expand` -- so the entry pairing it with `meow-digit-argument` never fires. meowr does what that entry says and treats the digit as a numeric argument, which is what the table was written to mean.
bof-shiftb2	hello world\nsecond line\nthird\n	0	-	-	-	B2	a digit with no selection. Meow raises "No selection": `meow--selection-fallback` looks the fallback up by `this-command`, which is `meow-expand-2`, while `meow-selection-command-fallback`'s key is `meow-expand` -- so the entry pairing it with `meow-digit-argument` never fires. meowr does what that entry says and treats the digit as a numeric argument, which is what the table was written to mean.
